---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout title="Otherwise Hyperarchive — Micol Gelsi">
<article class="hyperarchive" id="top">
  <div class="hyper-top"><span>EMAP RESIDENCY PROPOSAL / ARTISTIC RESEARCH / WEB + PUBLIC SPACE</span><a href={base}>← PORTFOLIO</a></div>
  <header class="hyper-hero"><div class="hyper-hero-stars" aria-hidden="true"><span>✳</span><span>★</span><span>✳</span><span>✦</span><span>★</span><span>✳</span><span>✦</span><span>★</span><span>✳</span><span>✦</span><span>★</span><span>✳</span></div><span>OTHERWISE HYPERARCHIVE / RESEARCH ATLAS / 2027</span><h1>MAP THE<br/>SUBTRACTED<span class="hyper-accent">_</span></h1><p>How can independent digital infrastructures support collective practices of mapping, documentation and spatial reappropriation?</p><small>CRITICAL MAPPING ★ ALLIED TECHNOLOGIES ★ COLLECTIVE MEDIA INFRASTRUCTURES</small></header>
  <nav class="hyper-nav" aria-label="Proposal sections"><a href="#concept">01 CONCEPT</a><a href="#untamed">02 PREVIOUS</a><a href="#system">03 SYSTEM</a><a href="#space">04 SPACE</a><a href="#loop">05 LOOP</a><a href="#process">06 PROCESS</a><a href="#references">07 REFERENCES</a></nav>
  <section id="concept" class="hyper-section"><span class="hyper-index">01 / RESEARCH QUESTION</span><div><h2>What does it mean to <em>map what is being subtracted?</em></h2><p>OTHERWISE HYPERARCHIVE investigates subtraction as a process affecting public space, collective memory, spontaneous ecologies and digital autonomy. Critical mapping examines mechanisms that regulate visibility, access and use, rather than recording loss as an isolated event.</p><p>Emerging from <em>Archive of the Untamed</em>, the project investigates what institutional classifications of urban space leave unrecorded: relationships of care, everyday forms of collective inhabitation and the memories carried by communities.</p><p>The research asks whether autonomous platforms and allied technologies can support situated documentation, intersectional struggles and collective spatial practice. It follows both the conditions of exclusion and the relationships that persist despite them.</p></div></section>
  <section id="untamed" class="hyper-section hyper-previous"><span class="hyper-index">02 / PREVIOUS</span><div><h2>From an urban archive to <em>a critical observatory.</em></h2><p>OTHERWISE HYPERARCHIVE emerges from a sequence of artistic practices, spatial conflicts and questions concerning the public use of the city. Its starting point is not an abstract model of an archive, but an existing research process.</p><div class="hyper-genealogy"><div class="hyper-genealogy-item"><span>01 / ARCHIVE OF THE UNTAMED</span><div><h3>Documenting what exceeds urban classification</h3><p>Archive of the Untamed investigates spontaneous urban ecologies, collective memory and informal practices of inhabiting the city. Its participatory web interface connects situated contributions across places, media and communities.</p></div></div><div class="hyper-genealogy-item"><span>02 / EVICTION AND SUBTRACTION</span><div><h3>The interruption of collective space</h3><p>Evictions, restrictions and the redefinition of urban access expose a conflict between lived uses of space and the frameworks that regulate them. The archive becomes a way to examine not only what disappears, but the processes through which space is made unavailable.</p></div></div><div class="hyper-genealogy-item"><span>03 / DECLARATIONS OF INTENT</span><div><h3>Legality, restoration and acceleration</h3><p>Institutional declarations concerning the restoration of legality and the acceleration of interventions form part of the critical context. The research asks how such language constructs urgency, authorises spatial transformation and frames collective occupation and use.</p></div></div><div class="hyper-genealogy-item"><span>04 / TOWARDS AN OBSERVATORY</span><div><h3>A network of situated witnesses</h3><p>The proposed independent observatory would connect archivists, documentary practitioners, artists and urban explorers through autonomous platforms and allied technologies, enabling shared observation and exchange. Rather than establishing a single authoritative account, it investigates how heterogeneous records and perspectives can remain connected and accessible.</p></div></div><div class="hyper-genealogy-item"><span>05 / A NETWORKED MEDIA ARCHIVE</span><div><h3>Occupation, reappropriation and artistic practice</h3><p>The observatory would support discussion of occupation, spatial reappropriation through art and the protection of contested places. A networked, independently maintained media archive — combining documentation, testimony, cartography, audiovisual material and experimental interfaces — would investigate the debounding of public/private space and intersecting struggles for access, ecology and collective life.</p></div></div></div><p class="hyper-pink-note">★ From recording places to examining the conditions under which they remain public.</p><div class="hyper-previous-links"><a href="https://thearchiveoftheuntamed.xyz/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span>01 / PREVIOUS RESEARCH</span>ARCHIVE OF THE UNTAMED ↗</a><span class="hyper-pending-link"><span>02 / PREVIOUS RESEARCH</span>ARCHIVE IS ALIVE <small>URL TO BE ADDED</small></span></div></div></section>
  <section id="system" class="hyper-section"><span class="hyper-index">03 / DIGITAL INFRASTRUCTURE</span><div><h2>Autonomous platforms, <em>allied technologies.</em></h2><p>The interface is treated as a political and spatial infrastructure. Independent platforms offer a site for investigating alternatives to commercial systems of hosting, classification and distribution. Who contributes, who controls access, who maintains the infrastructure, and how can records circulate without reproducing dominant hierarchies?</p><figure class="hyper-diagram"><figcaption>FIG. 01 — NETWORK / NON-HIERARCHICAL CONNECTIONS</figcaption><svg viewBox="0 0 900 380" role="img" aria-label="Rhizomatic archive network"><g class="wires"><path d="M450 184Q250 20 154 100M450 184Q680 12 758 92M450 184Q730 340 768 290M450 184Q210 365 145 292M154 100Q440 10 758 92M145 292Q460 330 768 290M154 100Q90 200 145 292M758 92Q850 180 768 290"/></g><g class="nodes"><circle cx="450" cy="184" r="51"/><circle cx="154" cy="100" r="35"/><circle cx="758" cy="92" r="35"/><circle cx="145" cy="292" r="35"/><circle cx="768" cy="290" r="35"/></g><g text-anchor="middle"><text x="450" y="169">HYPER</text><text x="450" y="181">ARCHIVE</text><text x="154" y="104">MEMORY</text><text x="758" y="96">ECOLOGIES</text><text x="145" y="296">SITES</text><text x="768" y="294">COMMUNITIES</text></g></svg></figure><p class="hyper-pink-note">★ Can technology act as an ally rather than an instrument of control?</p><p><strong>Allied technologies</strong> is a working proposition: tools whose design, maintenance and use are negotiated with the people and practices they support. Interfaces, mapping systems and media archives are considered adaptable and accountable research instruments.</p><small>Links are relational, revisable and multiple. Diagram is conceptual, not a data visualisation.</small></div></section>
  <section id="space" class="hyper-section"><span class="hyper-index">04 / SPATIAL TRANSLATION</span><div><h2>Return to <em>public space</em></h2><p>The research investigates the boundaries between public and private, online and offline, documentation and intervention. Projection mapping, temporary parasitic architectures and participatory construction translate media archives into spatial interfaces. These hybrid languages test how records are encountered, disputed and reinterpreted in the city.</p><div class="hyper-columns"><div><figure class="hyper-diagram"><figcaption>FIG. 02 — PROJECTION MAPPING</figcaption><svg viewBox="0 0 420 300" role="img" aria-label="Projection mapping onto a facade"><g class="wires"><path d="M85 72H315V258H85ZM112 98H176V154H112ZM220 98H283V154H220ZM112 180H176V236H112ZM220 180H283V236H220Z"/></g><g class="accent-wires"><path d="M30 265L112 98L283 154L390 265M30 265L112 236L283 98L390 265"/></g><text x="17" y="285">PROJECTOR</text></svg></figure><h3>01 / Projection mapping</h3><p>Archival fragments, cartographies and audiovisual contributions become temporary layers on existing urban surfaces.</p></div><div><figure class="hyper-diagram"><figcaption>FIG. 03 — PARASITIC ARCHITECTURE</figcaption><svg viewBox="0 0 420 300" role="img" aria-label="Temporary reversible architecture"><g class="wires"><path d="M35 55H170V265H35ZM60 80H145V160H60ZM0 265H420"/></g><g class="accent-wires"><path d="M170 120L250 85L360 135L280 170ZM170 120V225L280 275V170M280 275L360 230V135M250 85V195L360 230M170 225L250 195L280 275"/></g><text x="195" y="57">REVERSIBLE</text><text x="195" y="70">ATTACHMENT</text></svg></figure><h3>02 / Parasitic architectures</h3><p>Lightweight, reversible spatial devices respond to existing structures, opening possibilities for temporary gathering, interaction and collective use.</p></div></div><figure class="hyper-diagram"><figcaption>FIG. 04 — RECIPROCAL FEEDBACK / CURVED PATHS</figcaption><svg viewBox="0 0 900 260" role="img" aria-label="Curved reciprocal loop connecting archive, public interventions and new contributions"><g class="accent-wires"><path d="M180 130C280 12 370 12 450 130S640 248 730 130"/><path d="M730 130C650 8 555 8 450 130S280 252 180 130" stroke-dasharray="5 8"/><circle cx="180" cy="130" r="48"/><circle cx="450" cy="130" r="48"/><circle cx="730" cy="130" r="48"/></g><g text-anchor="middle"><text x="180" y="125">CRITICAL</text><text x="180" y="139">MAPPING</text><text x="450" y="125">DIGITAL</text><text x="450" y="139">AUTONOMY</text><text x="730" y="125">PUBLIC</text><text x="730" y="139">SPACE</text><text x="450" y="27">NEW TRACES / RETURN / REASSEMBLY</text></g></svg></figure></div></section>
  <section id="loop" class="hyper-section"><span class="hyper-index">05 / THE LOOP</span><div><h2>Map → reclaim → <em>return → map again</em></h2><p>A recurring process connects critical mapping, independent digital infrastructures and public-space interventions. Collective contributions inform the interface; spatial encounters generate new records and questions; these, in turn, alter the archive. The loop remains open to disagreement and revision.</p><div class="hyper-loop" aria-label="Critical mapping loop"><div class="hyper-loop-step"><span>01 ★ MAP THE SUBTRACTED</span><p>Identify processes of exclusion, erasure and control — and what persists despite them.</p></div><div class="hyper-loop-arrow">↓</div><div class="hyper-loop-step"><span>02 ★ RECLAIM THE DIGITAL</span><p>Develop independent interfaces and allied technologies with revisable access, classification and governance.</p></div><div class="hyper-loop-arrow">↓</div><div class="hyper-loop-step"><span>03 ★ RETURN TO PUBLIC SPACE</span><p>Projection mapping, parasitic architectures and situated encounters.</p></div><div class="hyper-loop-return">↖ NEW TRACES / NEW CONFLICTS / MAP AGAIN ★</div></div></div></section>
  <section id="process" class="hyper-section"><span class="hyper-index">06 / TWO-MONTH RESIDENCY</span><div><h2>Research through collaboration and prototyping</h2><div class="hyper-steps"><p><strong>W01–02</strong>Local research, encounters with communities and collaborating artist; collective assessment of the existing archive.</p><p><strong>W03–04</strong>Participatory interface redesign, critical mapping, sketches and spatial prototypes.</p><p><strong>W05–06</strong>Web development, fabrication, projection tests and digital–physical integration.</p><p><strong>W07–08</strong>Situated testing, adaptation, documentation and public presentation.</p></div><div class="hyper-columns"><div><h3>Artist contribution</h3><p>Web development, creative coding, audiovisual production, projection-mapping workflow and laptop.</p></div><div><h3>Host / local collaboration</h3><p>Access to fabrication tools and materials, projector and audiovisual equipment, technical support, local networks and site permissions where required.</p></div></div><small>Intended outcomes: a functional, collectively usable iteration of the archive and at least one site-responsive interactive installation. Final format remains responsive to local context and collaboration.</small></div></section>
  <section id="references" class="hyper-section"><span class="hyper-index">07 / SELECTED REFERENCES</span><div><h2>Conceptual coordinates</h2><ol><li>Gilles Deleuze & Félix Guattari, <em>A Thousand Plateaus: Capitalism and Schizophrenia</em> (1980; English translation 1987). — Rhizomatic thinking.</li><li>Donna J. Haraway, <em>Staying with the Trouble: Making Kin in the Chthulucene</em> (2016). — Situated relationships and more-than-human worlds.</li><li>Stefano Harney & Fred Moten, <em>The Undercommons: Fugitive Planning & Black Study</em> (2013). — Collective study and self-organisation.</li><li>Stavros Stavrides, <em>Common Space: The City as Commons</em> (2016). — Commoning and shared urban space.</li><li>Shannon Mattern, <em>A City Is Not a Computer: Other Urban Intelligences</em> (2021). — Critical approaches to urban data and infrastructure.</li></ol><small>Research background: <a href="https://thearchiveoftheuntamed.xyz/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Archive of the Untamed</a>.</small></div></section>
  <footer class="hyper-footer"><span>OTHERWISE HYPERARCHIVE / WORKING PROPOSAL / 2027</span><a href="#top">BACK TO TOP ↑</a></footer>
</article>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
.hyperarchive{--hyper-line:var(--line,#555);--hyper-accent:var(--signal,#ff71c3);width:100%;font:12px/1.65 "Courier New",monospace;color:var(--fg)}
.hyperarchive *{box-sizing:border-box}.hyperarchive a{color:inherit}.hyper-top,.hyper-footer{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px;padding:14px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--hyper-line);font-size:10px}.hyper-hero{padding:70px 0 55px;min-height:52vh;display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:space-between;border-bottom:1px solid var(--hyper-line)}.hyper-hero>span,.hyper-hero small,.hyper-index,.hyper-diagram figcaption,.hyperarchive small{font-size:10px;letter-spacing:.07em;opacity:.75}.hyper-hero h1{font:400 clamp(34px,6.3vw,110px)/.96 "Courier New",monospace;letter-spacing:-.075em;margin:35px 0;overflow-wrap:anywhere}.hyper-accent{color:var(--hyper-accent)}.hyper-hero p{font-size:clamp(13px,1.4vw,17px)}.hyper-nav{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:18px;padding:16px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--hyper-line)}.hyper-section{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(130px,24%) minmax(0,1fr);gap:24px;padding:34px 0 48px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--hyper-line)}.hyper-section>div{max-width:1000px}.hyper-section h2{font:400 clamp(18px,2.2vw,30px)/1.2 "Courier New",monospace;margin:0 0 24px}.hyper-section h3{font:400 12px "Courier New",monospace;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0 0 12px}.hyper-section p{font:inherit;max-width:660px;margin:0 0 15px}.hyper-columns{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:20px}.hyper-diagram{position:relative;border:1px solid var(--hyper-line);margin:22px 0;overflow:hidden;padding:28px 6px 6px}.hyper-diagram figcaption{position:absolute;top:10px;left:12px}.hyper-diagram svg{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;min-height:160px}.hyper-diagram svg text{fill:var(--fg);font:10px "Courier New",monospace}.hyper-diagram .wires{stroke:var(--fg);opacity:.5;stroke-dasharray:3 7;fill:none}.hyper-diagram .nodes{stroke:var(--hyper-accent);fill:var(--bg);stroke-width:1.2}.hyper-diagram .accent-wires{fill:none;stroke:var(--hyper-accent);stroke-width:1.5}.hyper-steps{margin:30px 0}.hyper-steps p{display:grid;grid-template-columns:75px 1fr;gap:10px;border-top:1px solid var(--hyper-line);padding:12px 0;margin:0}.hyper-steps strong{color:var(--hyper-accent);font-weight:400}.hyper-section ol{padding-left:20px}.hyper-section li{margin:0 0 10px}.hyper-footer{border:0;margin-top:30px}.hyperarchive :target{scroll-margin-top:24px}
html[data-portfolio-mode="institutional"] .hyperarchive{--hyper-accent:#222;--hyper-line:#aaa}
@media(max-width:760px){.hyper-section{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:15px}.hyper-columns{grid-template-columns:1fr}.hyper-hero{min-height:40vh;padding:40px 0}.hyper-top,.hyper-footer{flex-direction:column}}

.hyper-hero{position:relative;isolation:isolate;overflow:hidden}.hyper-hero>span,.hyper-hero>h1,.hyper-hero>p,.hyper-hero>small{position:relative;z-index:1}.hyper-hero-stars{position:absolute;inset:0;pointer-events:none;z-index:0}.hyper-hero-stars span{position:absolute;font:clamp(16px,3vw,42px)/1 monospace;color:var(--hyper-accent);opacity:.65;animation:hyper-twinkle 4s ease-in-out infinite alternate}.hyper-hero-stars span:nth-child(1){left:8%;top:18%;animation-delay:-.3s}.hyper-hero-stars span:nth-child(2){left:84%;top:12%;animation-delay:-2s}.hyper-hero-stars span:nth-child(3){left:69%;top:44%;animation-delay:-1s}.hyper-hero-stars span:nth-child(4){left:94%;top:68%;animation-delay:-3s}.hyper-hero-stars span:nth-child(5){left:35%;top:8%;animation-delay:-2.6s}.hyper-hero-stars span:nth-child(6){left:55%;top:82%;animation-delay:-1.5s}.hyper-hero-stars span:nth-child(7){left:14%;top:76%;animation-delay:-.8s}.hyper-hero-stars span:nth-child(8){left:75%;top:90%;animation-delay:-3.3s}.hyper-hero-stars span:nth-child(9){left:96%;top:29%;animation-delay:-1.8s}.hyper-hero-stars span:nth-child(10){left:44%;top:54%;animation-delay:-2.8s}.hyper-hero-stars span:nth-child(11){left:3%;top:49%;animation-delay:-.9s}.hyper-hero-stars span:nth-child(12){left:61%;top:14%;animation-delay:-3.6s}@keyframes hyper-twinkle{0%{transform:scale(.65) rotate(-12deg);opacity:.2}100%{transform:scale(1.35) rotate(18deg);opacity:.9}}
.hyper-loop{border:1px solid var(--hyper-line);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,36px);margin-top:24px}.hyper-loop-step{border-top:1px solid var(--hyper-line);padding:16px 0}.hyper-loop-step span{font-size:clamp(13px,2vw,22px);color:var(--hyper-accent)}.hyper-loop-step p{margin:8px 0 0}.hyper-loop-arrow{font-size:28px;color:var(--hyper-accent);text-align:center}.hyper-loop-return{border-top:1px dashed var(--hyper-accent);padding:18px 0 0;color:var(--hyper-accent);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.05em}@media(prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.hyper-hero-stars span{animation:none!important}}

/* A little pink handwriting-like interruption to the otherwise austere mono layout. */
.hyperarchive{--hyper-pink:#ff78bd;--hyper-green:#b6ff00}
.hyper-section h2 em,.hyper-untamed p em{font-family:Georgia,"Times New Roman",serif!important;font-style:italic!important;font-weight:400;color:var(--hyper-pink);letter-spacing:-.025em}
.hyper-pink-note{font-family:Georgia,"Times New Roman",serif!important;font-size:clamp(18px,2.2vw,27px)!important;font-style:italic!important;color:var(--hyper-pink);max-width:640px}
.hyper-external{display:inline-block;border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;margin-top:12px;text-decoration:none}
.hyperarchive .hyper-diagram .accent-wires path{stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.hyperarchive .hyper-loop{border-radius:48% 8% 35% 6%/5% 30% 6% 20%;overflow:hidden}
.hyperarchive .hyper-loop-step{border-radius:60% 0 0 0/12% 0 0 0}
@media(prefers-reduced-motion:no-preference){.hyper-bitmap-fallback{animation:hyper-bitmap-drift 7s ease-in-out infinite alternate}@keyframes hyper-bitmap-drift{to{transform:translate(8px,-9px) rotate(5deg)}}}

/* Previous research: text-first, restrained, with no photographic effects. */
.hyper-previous-links{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:12px;margin-top:32px}
.hyper-previous-links>a,.hyper-pending-link{display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px;min-height:115px;padding:14px;border:1px solid var(--hyper-line);font:400 clamp(12px,1.6vw,17px)/1.3 "Courier New",monospace;text-decoration:none}
.hyper-previous-links>a:hover{border-color:var(--hyper-accent);color:var(--hyper-accent)}
.hyper-previous-links :is(a,.hyper-pending-link)>span{font-size:10px;opacity:.65}
.hyper-pending-link{opacity:.6}
.hyper-pending-link small{font-size:9px}
@media(max-width:600px){.hyper-previous-links{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
.hyper-genealogy{margin:30px 0}.hyper-genealogy-item{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(145px,28%) 1fr;gap:20px;padding:22px 0;border-top:1px solid var(--hyper-line)}.hyper-genealogy-item>span{font:11px/1.4 "Courier New",monospace;color:var(--hyper-accent)}.hyper-genealogy-item h3{font-size:clamp(15px,1.6vw,20px);text-transform:none;margin-bottom:10px}.hyper-genealogy-item p{margin-bottom:0}.hyper-genealogy-item:nth-child(even){padding-left:clamp(0px,2vw,20px)}@media(max-width:640px){.hyper-genealogy-item{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:9px}.hyper-genealogy-item:nth-child(even){padding-left:0}}
</style>

